text:
The paper introduces \textsf{MProtect}\xspace, a system designed to protect sensitive process data from the operating system (OS). The system is built on two key insights: controlling the OS's view of virtual memory and ensuring the integrity of the interrupt vector table. \textsf{MProtect}\xspace achieves this by employing a software Trusted Computing Base (TCB) called \textsf{Guardian}\xspace, which runs at a higher privilege level and intercepts sensitive operations. Unlike traditional hypervisors, \textsf{Guardian}\xspace is minimalistic, focusing on protecting itself and mediating specific actions on behalf of the OS.

\subsection{\textsf{Guardian}\xspace: The Minimalistic TCB}
\label{sec:guardiandesign}
The \textsf{Guardian}\xspace is a lightweight software TCB that operates at a higher privilege level, similar to a hypervisor. It is designed to be non-intrusive, only intercepting sensitive operations without managing resources or handling exceptions for protected processes. This approach significantly reduces the code size compared to traditional hypervisors. The \textsf{Guardian}\xspace is re-entrant and can handle concurrent invocations from multiple threads across different cores.

The \textsf{Guardian}\xspace has a narrow Application Binary Interface (ABI) with seven entry points, including handling virtual memory control register updates, triggers, and OS-initiated operations. It also tracks physical frame information and sensitive process information, such as page table base addresses, user-space trampolines, secret keys, capability lists, and execution contexts.

\subsection{Design of \textsf{MProtect}\xspace}
\label{sec:design}
\textsf{MProtect}\xspace is designed to secure two key abstractions for sensitive processes: virtual memory and virtual interpreter. The system uses encrypted views for non-semantic OS accesses and a lightweight capability system to mediate semantic accesses. It also concerns the control-flow integrity of sensitive processes through a secure virtual interpreter.

\subsection{Key Abstractions}
\label{sec:key_abstractions}
\textsf{MProtect}\xspace introduces two key abstractions: secure virtual memory and secure virtual interpreter. The secure virtual memory provides an encrypted view of user space for the OS, while the secure virtual interpreter protects the execution context of sensitive processes.

\subsection{Coherent Encrypted View}
\label{sec:dual_view}
\textsf{MProtect}\xspace provides a coherent encrypted view of user space to the OS, ensuring that any changes made by the process are reflected in the encrypted view. This is achieved by marking double-mapped pages as read-only, causing a permission fault when the process writes to such a page. The \textsf{Guardian}\xspace then invalidates the corresponding entry in the encrypted view, allowing the write to proceed.

\subsection{Key Insights}
\label{sec:design-insight}
\textsf{MProtect}\xspace's design is based on two key insights: controlling the OS's view of virtual memory and ensuring the integrity of the interrupt vector table. By depriving the OS of capabilities to bypass the MMU and arbitrarily modify page tables, \textsf{MProtect}\xspace "blindfolds" the OS, forcing it to use a cloak page table for non-semantic access. Additionally, \textsf{MProtect}\xspace ensures its own version of the interrupt vector table is used when an exception interrupts a sensitive process.

\subsection{Innovations and Contributions}
The innovative aspects of \textsf{MProtect}\xspace include its minimalistic \textsf{Guardian}\xspace, which reduces the TCB size and complexity compared to prior works. The system also leverages user-space trampolines to reduce the changes required in the OS, further simplifying the implementation. \textsf{MProtect}\xspace's design allows for secure data movement by the OS while maintaining coherence between the encrypted and user views, providing a more efficient and secure approach to protecting sensitive process data.
